# Project Instructions for Forge Agent
# Copy this file to FORGE.md in your workspace root and fill it in.
# Forge reads it automatically and injects it into every prompt.
# Keep it focused — every token here costs context on every turn.

## Stack
<!-- What languages, frameworks, build tools, and runtimes this project uses -->
TypeScript + Node.js. esbuild for bundling. Vitest for tests.

## Workspace Layout
<!-- Top-level directories the agent needs to know about -->
src/          — all TypeScript source
src/backend/  — backend lifecycle and adapters
src/llm/      — LLM dispatch, templates, prompt injection
src/tools/    — tool definitions and execution
src/sidebar/  — VS Code webview and agent loop
src/config/   — Zod config schema and loader
config/       — user-facing config examples and templates
tests/        — unit and integration tests

## Key Files
<!-- The single most important owner for each concern — agent starts here -->
- Entry point:       src/extension.ts
- Agent loop:        src/sidebar/AgentLoop.ts
- LLM dispatch:      src/llm/ChatClient.ts
- Tool definitions:  src/tools/registerAllTools.ts
- Config schema:     src/config/schema.ts
- System prompt:     src/templates/builtin/execute.njk

## Navigation Rules
- Grep before creating — check if a function or file already exists
- New backend logic → src/backend/, new tools → src/tools/, new LLM logic → src/llm/
- Do not put business logic in src/extension.ts — it is wiring only

## Hard Stops
- No destructive shell commands (rm -rf, git reset --hard) without explicit user confirmation
- No cloud API calls or outbound network requests beyond what the user configured
- No hardcoded paths, secrets, or API keys

## Code Style
- TypeScript strict mode — no `any` without a comment explaining why
- Zod for all config and external input validation
- Dispose VS Code resources in deactivate() or via context.subscriptions
